*Context: *China=E2=80=99s peak recruitment season, which spans the months =
of
September and October, is ending soon, and new graduates are getting
nervous about their job prospects. In this week=E2=80=99s feature translati=
on (link
to *original
<https://substack.com/redirect/5cd7ac72-88e9-464c-8993-606a9c7d492f?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
we hear from Chinese AI PhD students and graduates who feel like they are
getting left behind. This is my first time translating an article from
AI=E6=95=85=E4=BA=8B=E8=AE=A1=E5=88=92, a smaller WeChat public account tha=
t publishes human-interest
stories about AI. Its posts only get a couple hundred views, but this one
was reposted by Renwu, which publishes longform essays and investigations
that have featured in previous issues (e.g., *ChinAI #111
<https://substack.com/redirect/78e2652c-5564-4060-8497-a3450753f528?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>)*

*Key Passages: Haoran Wang is a PhD candidate at a very good (top 40) but
not elite university in Shanghai, where he specializes in large
models. *However,
since his university doesn=E2=80=99t rank in the top tier and he has only p=
ublished
two papers at top conferences, he was rejected by multiple companies during
last year=E2=80=99s fall recruitment.

   -

   Wang considers himself a =E2=80=9Cmediocre=E2=80=9D [=E5=B9=B3=E5=BA=B8]=
 PhD student. Students like him
   face a dilemma: the big tech giants can=E2=80=99t hire them because they=
 don=E2=80=99t meet
   the high bar; the smaller-sized companies can=E2=80=99t afford to raise =
them
   because these firms have to prioritize cost-effectiveness.
   -

   Let=E2=80=99s start with the big tech giants. Alibaba=E2=80=99s T-Star p=
rogram,
   ByteDance=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9CTop Seed=E2=80=9D program, and Huawei=E2=80=
=99s =E2=80=9CTopMinds=E2=80=9D program scoop up
   the best PhD grads with this package: *=E2=80=9Cresearch freedom, priori=
ty
   resources, and annual salaries exceeding one million yuan*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   From the article: =E2=80=9C*On social media, Wang frequently encounters
   =E2=80=98Versailles=E2=80=99 posts from his peers. In these posts, outst=
anding AI PhDs are
   torn between the =E2=80=98happy agony=E2=80=99 of taking a million-yuan =
annual salary to
   work at a large company or remaining in academia as a university
professor*.=E2=80=9D
   We all know these types of people who complain about their champagne
   problems.
   -

   But Wang is also struggling to land with small and medium-sized
   companies. His expertise in AI coding has led to interviews for position=
s
   in healthcare and finance, but =E2=80=9C*when the interviewers realized =
he
   needed time to familiarize himself with specific business scenarios, the=
y
   stopped the process*.=E2=80=9D These smaller firms can=E2=80=99t justify=
 paying both for
   expensive talent and also the time to accumulate business familiarity.

*Yifan Zhang, a second-year PhD student in computer vision, is grappling
with the high barrier to entry to future jobs. *The article relates, =E2=80=
=9C*He
saw in public job postings that companies required AI PhDs to have one or
two first-author papers at top conferences. However, job-hunting colleagues
told Zhang that if he hadn=E2=80=99t participated in high-profile industry
projects, he would need at least five papers to get an interview. From the
industry perspective, this is one of the few quantifiable criteria for
measuring a PhD=E2=80=99s ability*.=E2=80=9D

   -

   The requirement of multiple published papers at top venues has led to
   these top conferences being flooded with submissions, overwhelming the
   review process. Yiping Lu, a Northwestern University professor, made wav=
es
   when he shared a reviewer comment (screenshot below) on his NeurIPS pape=
r
   submission. =E2=80=9CAdam=E2=80=9D, which the reviewer seems to not reco=
gnize, is a
   commonly used optimization algorithm. This *Twitter post
   <https://substack.com/redirect/5fd1a60e-a5a4-402e-86e4-c629d894763b?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   attracted 620.8k reads and 749 retweets.

<https://substack.com/redirect/7efcb7bb-df58-4a42-8fc9-5a2b65784d26?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>

   -

   The paper threshold also produces a mismatch between PhD training and
   useful industry skills. The article continues Zhang=E2=80=99s story: =E2=
=80=9C*After
   learning about large models during his internship, Zhang considered
   adjusting his research direction. However, when his supervisor stated, =
=E2=80=98Six
   research publications are required for full-time employment,=E2=80=99 he=
 decided to
   focus on publishing papers in his familiar field. Although this research
   had limited significance for business applications, they were a rigid
   threshold for employment*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   This mismatch relates to a point I emphasize in my book=E2=80=99s case s=
tudy of
   the Second Industrial Revolution and why the U.S.=E2=80=99s mechanical e=
ngineering
   education system was more effective than that of Germany. I argue that
   German institutions =E2=80=9Cprioritized scientific and theoretical educ=
ation at
   the expense of practical skills,=E2=80=9D which led to =E2=80=9Cweak lin=
kages between
   mechanical engineering education and industrial applications.=E2=80=9D (=
figure
   below from p. 121 of *Technology and the Rise of Great Powers*)

<https://substack.com/redirect/52252717-28ef-4066-9786-0f6e202703d0?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>

   -

   So much of the discussion and analysis of talent competition in AI
   remains at such a surface level: who has more! The stories in this week=
=E2=80=99s
   translation remind us to dig a little bit deeper.

